 ## Overview
-Frequency Regularization (FR) stabilizes the optimization of 3D Gaussian Splatting by minimizing the spectral discrepancy between rendered images and ground truth, specifically addressing the problem of over-reconstruction where redundant Gaussians form in already well-reconstructed regions [S2]. Unlike naive pixel-wise L1 loss, which yields small gradients in over-reconstructed areas and stalls densification, FR increases gradients in these regions by explicitly disentangling amplitude and phase components, thereby guiding adaptive densification toward high-frequency detail [S2].
+Frequency Regularization (FR) is a training-time constraint for 3D Gaussian Splatting that minimizes the discrepancy between the frequency spectrum of rendered images and ground truth to alleviate over-reconstruction and improve Gaussian densification [S2]. Unlike naive pixel-wise L1 loss, which results in small gradients in over-reconstructed regions and prevents effective densification, FR explicitly disentangles frequency components to increase pixel gradients in these regions, enabling adaptive densification [S2]. It builds on [Regularization and Geometry Constraints](6269-regularization-and-geometry-constraints.md) by adding a frequency-domain loss term to the total training loss.
 
