 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The addition of 2D FFT and inverse operations (if used for annealing) per iteration increases computational overhead compared to pure spatial L1 loss. FFT complexity is $O(N \log N)$ where $N = H \times W$.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing complex frequency matrices of size $H \times W$ for both rendered and ground truth images.
-*   **Measured Performance:** In the "Bicycle" scene, FR significantly increased pixel gradients within over-reconstruction regions compared to the baseline method (equivalent to 3D-GS with L1 loss), enabling better adaptive densification [S2].
-*   **Strengths:** Effectively alleviates over-reconstruction; improves Gaussian densification by revealing over-reconstructed regions that L1 loss misses; pushes Gaussians toward high-frequency regions [S2][S3, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Naive application can bias optimization toward artifacts if frequency bands are not annealed [S2].
-*   **When to use:** When the scene exhibits over-reconstruction (blurry or redundant Gaussians in simple areas) or under-reconstruction in detail-rich regions.
-*   **When not to use:** When computational budget is extremely tight and spatial L1 loss is sufficient for coarse reconstruction.
+*   **Time Complexity:** The addition of DFT and inverse DFT operations increases computational overhead per iteration compared to standard L1 loss. The complexity is dominated by the FFT, $O(HW \log(HW))$ per channel.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing frequency domain representations for rendered and ground truth images, adding $O(HW)$ memory overhead.
+*   **Measured Performance:** In the 'Bicycle' scene, FR significantly increases pixel gradients within over-reconstruction regions compared to the baseline method without FR [S2]. It allows for superior Gaussian densification when combined with frequency annealing [S2].
+*   **Strengths:** Effectively alleviates over-reconstruction, improves Gaussian densification by revealing over-reconstructed regions, and handles amplitude and phase information separately for more accurate disparity assessment [S2].
+*   **Limitations:** Without frequency annealing, it can still lead to restricted densification and artifacts [S2]. It requires careful tuning of frequency filters if annealing is used.
+*   **When to use:** When over-reconstruction is prevalent, or when standard L1 loss fails to drive densification in specific regions.
+*   **When not to use:** When computational resources are extremely limited and the overhead of FFT is prohibitive, or when the scene is already well-reconstructed without artifacts.
 
 ## Background
-Frequency Regularization was introduced as a method to improve 3D Gaussian Splatting optimization by addressing convergence speed, visual artifacts, and improper Gaussians [S1]. It was notably implemented in "FreGS: 3D Gaussian Splatting with Progressive Frequency Regularization" [S2], which demonstrated that minimizing the discrepancy between the frequency spectrum of rendered images and ground truth alleviates over-reconstruction. The technique is part of a broader family of regularization methods including geometry constraints and depth supervision [S1].
+Frequency Regularization was introduced to address convergence speed, visual artifacts, and improper Gaussians in 3D Gaussian Splatting optimization [S1]. It is part of the broader direction of "Additional Regularization" in 3D GS improvements, alongside geometry-aware approaches [S1]. The specific formulation using amplitude and phase discrepancy was proposed in FreGS [S2]. It builds on the observation that pixel-wise L1 loss cannot differentiate between over-reconstructed and well-reconstructed regions, leading to redundant Gaussian creation [S2].
 
